       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Amlodipine 20 mg is double the FDA-labeled maximum of 10 mg once daily for adults. Doses above 10 mg raise the risk of peripheral edema, hypotension, and reflex tachycardia, and they don't add much blood pressure control. Nothing in the patient details you gave (normal kidney and liver function, 70 kg) would justify going above the maximum.\n\nHere are some options:\n\n- **Amlodipine 10 mg PO once daily.** This is the maximum standard dose. If you're titrating, the usual start is 5 mg daily (2.5 mg for frail or older patients, or those with hepatic impairment). It can go up to 10 mg after 1\u20132 weeks.\n- **Amlodipine 5 mg PO once daily.** This is a typical starting dose.\n- **A second agent.** If 10 mg isn't controlling the blood pressure, adding an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide-like diuretic is usually better than raising the amlodipine dose.\n\nWas 20 mg a typo, for example meant to be 10 mg or 2.5 mg? Or is there a reason for it that I should know about, such as an attending's instruction? If so, please confirm with your attending or a pharmacist. Tell me the dose you want and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
